<h2>Wind Versus Water Denials on the Coast</h2>
<p>Corpus Christi and the surrounding barrier islands face a dispute that inland cities rarely do: whether hurricane damage was caused by wind or by storm surge. Many coastal homeowners carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA windstorm policy, and a separate flood policy. The <a href="https://www.twia.org/about-us/overview/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Windstorm Insurance Association</a> covers wind and hail losses only, and homeowners policies generally exclude flood. When each insurer attributes the damage to the other peril, owners can be left with a denial from one and a partial payment from the other.</p>
<p>Evidence matters here. Photos of roof and window openings above interior water damage, the height of water lines, debris lines, and weather and tide records for the date of loss can help show which force caused which damage. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage claims</a> explains how these disputes typically develop, and the <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page covers the broader process.</p>

