<h2>Partial Denials Deserve the Same Attention</h2>
<p>Not every denial arrives as a single letter rejecting the whole claim. Often the insurer accepts part of the loss and denies the rest: it pays for a few roof slopes but denies interior water damage, or covers the fence but excludes the detached garage. Because a partial denial comes with a check, it is easy to overlook. Read each payment letter for the items or areas the insurer has declined, and treat those items as a denial that may need a response. A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can map each denied item to the policy language cited and the evidence available, so you can see which parts of the claim are worth pursuing.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take After a Denial</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get the denial in writing.</strong> Note the date you received it. Several deadlines can run from that date.</li>
<li><strong>Request the file.</strong> Ask for the policy provisions cited and copies of any engineer, inspector, or adjuster reports the insurer relied on.</li>
<li><strong>Read your full policy,</strong> including endorsements and the declarations page. Exclusions are often modified by endorsements.</li>
<li><strong>Gather independent evidence.</strong> That may include a detailed inspection, contractor findings, and NOAA weather records. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> can help you find reported events near your property.</li>
<li><strong>Respond in writing.</strong> If you disagree, send a written request for reconsideration with your supporting evidence, and keep proof of delivery.</li>
<li><strong>Track deadlines.</strong> Policy deadlines, TWIA statutory deadlines, and deadlines to sue can all apply. Consult a Texas attorney about any deadline to file suit.</li>
</ol>

<h2>TWIA Denials Follow a Separate Process</h2>
<p>If TWIA denied all or part of your windstorm claim, Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210, Subchapter L-1, sets out the dispute process. Under the <a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.2210.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">statute</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li>A claimant who wants to contest a denial must notify TWIA of the intent to sue before the limitations period runs out. Missing this notice waives the right to contest the denial.</li>
<li>TWIA may require mediation or a moderated settlement conference and must request it within 60 days of receiving the notice. That process must be completed within 60 days of the request unless extended.</li>
<li>Suit must be filed no later than two years after the claimant receives the partial or full denial notice. This is a statute of repose.</li>
<li>The general Chapter 542A pre-suit notice does not apply to actions against TWIA.</li>
</ul>
<p>These rules are strict, and public adjusters cannot give legal advice. If your TWIA claim was denied, talk with a Texas attorney about the deadlines early. Our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page and the <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> can help you see the general timeline.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster Can Help With a Denied Claim</h2>
<p>We start by reading the denial and the policy side by side, so you know what the insurer is relying on. Then we inspect the property, document the damage with photos, measurements, and where helpful drone imagery or Matterport 3D scans, and compare what we find with the insurer's reports. If the evidence supports a different conclusion, we prepare a written response and a line-item Xactimate estimate and present them to the insurer.</p>
<p>Sometimes the honest answer is that the denial is consistent with the policy. When that happens, we tell you. When a dispute calls for a lawyer, such as a coverage lawsuit or a bad-faith question, we recommend you consult a Texas attorney. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>If the insurer accepted part of the claim and paid too little, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>. To have your denial reviewed, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
